## 2. Easy practical code to remember

I've simplified the code from your PDF. The important points remain the same: HTML inputs, JavaScript functions, button clicks, formulas, and displaying the output.

You can copy this into a file named `experiment1.html` and open it in a browser.

```

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Area Calculator</title>
</head>
<body>

<h3>Area of Triangle</h3>
Base: <input type="number" id="base">
Height: <input type="number" id="height">
<button onclick="triangle()">Calculate</button>
<p id="result1"></p>

<h3>Area of Rectangle</h3>
Length: <input type="number" id="length">
Width: <input type="number" id="width">
<button onclick="rectangle()">Calculate</button>
<p id="result2"></p>

<h3>Area of Circle</h3>
Radius: <input type="number" id="radius">
<button onclick="circle()">Calculate</button>
<p id="result3"></p>

<script>
function triangle() {
    let b = Number(document.getElementById("base").value);
    let h = Number(document.getElementById("height").value);
    let area = (b * h) / 2;
    document.getElementById("result1").innerHTML = area;
}

function rectangle() {
    let l = Number(document.getElementById("length").value);
    let w = Number(document.getElementById("width").value);
    let area = l * w;
    document.getElementById("result2").innerHTML = area;
}

function circle() {
    let r = Number(document.getElementById("radius").value);
    let area = 3.14 * r * r;
    document.getElementById("result3").innerHTML = area;
}
</script>

</body>
</html>

```

### 3. Understand the code line by line

Don't try to memorize the entire code at once. Understand these five important parts.

### Part 1: Take input

```
<input type="number" id="base">
```

This creates a box where the user enters a number.

- `type="number"` allows numeric input.
- `id="base"` gives the input a unique name.
  ### Part 2: Create a button

```
<button onclick="triangle()">Calculate</button>
```

When the user clicks the button, the `triangle()` function runs.

Remember: `onclick` means when clicked.

### Part 3: Get the input value

```
let b = Number(document.getElementById("base").value);
```

In simple words:

- `getElementById("base")` finds the input box.
- `.value` gets the entered value.
- `Number()` converts it into a number.
- `let b` stores that number in variable `b`.
  ### Part 4: Calculate the area

```
let area = (b * h) / 2;
```

This applies the triangle formula and stores the answer in `area`.

### Part 5: Display the result

```
document.getElementById("result1").innerHTML = area;
```

This displays the calculated answer on the webpage inside the element whose ID is `result1`.

Easy memory trick: Input → Button → Function → Formula → Output.

The rectangle and circle functions follow exactly the same pattern. Only the input names and formulas change.

